If now the base {EH|pEH} equals the base {NQ|pNQ}, and the solid {AB|sAB} equals the solid {CD|sCD}, then {CM} equal {AG}. For parallelepipedal solids of the same height are to one another as the bases, and the base {EH|pEH} is to {NQ|pNQ} as {CM} is to {AG}, and it is clear that in the parallelepipedal solids {AB|sAB} and {CD|sCD} the bases are reciprocally proportional to the heights. [!just XI.32]

Next, let the base {EH|pEH} not be equal to the base {NQ|pNQ}, but let {EH|pEH} be greater.

Now the solid {AB|sAB} equals the solid {CD|sCD}, therefore {CM} is also greater than {AG}.

Make {CT} equal to {AG}, complete the parallelepipedal solid {VC|sCV} on {NQ|pNQ} as base with {CT} as height. [!just I.3, I.31]

Now, since the solid {AB|sAB} equals the solid {CD|sCD}, and {CV|sCV} is outside them, and equals have to the same the same ratio, therefore the solid {AB|sAB} is to the solid {CV|sCV} as the solid {CD|sCD} is to the solid {CV|sCV}. [!just V.7]

But the solid {AB|sAB} is to the solid *CY* as the base {EH|pEH} is to the base {NQ|pNQ}, for the solids {AB|sAB} and {CV|sCV} are of equal height, and the solid {CD|sCD} is to the solid {CV|sCV} as the base {MQ|pMQ} is to the base {TQ|pTQ} and {CM} is to {CT}, therefore the base {EH|pEH} is to the base {NQ|pNQ} as {MC|CM} is to {CT}. [!just XI.32; XI.25; VI.1]

But {CT} equals {AG}, therefore the base {EH|pEH} is to the base {NQ|pNQ} as {MC|CM} is to {AG}.

Therefore in the parallelepipedal solids {AB|sAB} and {CD|sCD} the bases are reciprocally proportional to the heights.

Again, in the parallelepipedal solids {AB|sAB} and {CD|sCD} let the bases be reciprocally proportional to the heights, that is the base {EH|pEH} is to the base {NQ|pNQ}, so let the height of the solid {CD|sCD} be to the height of the solid {AB|sAB}.

I say that the solid {AB|sAB} equals the solid {CD|sCD}.

Let the sides which stand up be at right angles to the bases.

Now, if the base {EH|pEH} equals the base {NQ|pNQ}, and the base {EH|pEH} is to the base {NQ|pNQ} as the height of the solid {CD|sCD} is to the height of the solid {AB|sAB}, therefore the height of the solid {CD|sCD} also equals the height of the solid {AB|sAB}.

But parallelepipedal solids on equal bases and of the same height equal one another, therefore the solid {AB|sAB} equals the solid {CD|sCD}. [!just XI.31]

Next, let the base {EH|pEH} not be equal to the base {NQ|pNQ}, but let {EH|pEH} be greater.

Therefore the height of the solid {CD|sCD} is also greater than the height of the solid {AB|sAB}, that is, {CM} is greater than {AG}.

Make {CT} equal to {AG} again, and complete the solid {CV|sCV}. [!just I.3, I.31]

Since the base {EH|pEH} is to the base {NQ|pNQ} as {MC|CM} is to {AG}, and {AG} equals {CT}, therefore the base {EH|pEH} is to the base {NQ|pNQ} as {CM} is to {CT}.

But the base {EH|pEH} is to the base {NQ|pNQ} as the solid {AB|sAB} is to the solid {CV|sCV}, for the solids {AB|sAB} and {CV|sCV} are of equal height, and {CM} is to {CT} as the base {MQ|pMQ} is to the base {QT|pTQ} and as the solid {CD|sCD} is to the solid {CV|sCV}. [!just XI.32, VI.1; XI.25]

Therefore the solid {AB|sAB} is to the solid {CV|sCV} as the solid {CD|sCD} to the solid {CV|sCV}. Therefore each of the solids {AB|sAB} and {CD|sCD} has to {CV|sCV} the same ratio. Therefore the solid {AB|sAB} equals the solid {CD|sCD}. [!just V.9]

<hr width="40%">

Now let the sides which stand up, {FE|EF:canvas_1}, {BL:canvas_1}, {GA|AG:canvas_1}, {HK:canvas_1}, {ON:canvas_1}, {DP:canvas_1}, {MC|CM:canvas_1}, and {RQ|QR:canvas_1}, not be at right angles to their bases. Draw perpendiculars from the points {F:canvas_1}, {G:canvas_1}, {B:canvas_1}, {K:canvas_1}, {O:canvas_1}, {M:canvas_1}, {D:canvas_1}, and {R:canvas_1} to the planes through {EH|pEH:canvas_1} and {NQ|pNQ:canvas_1}, and let them meet the planes at {S:canvas_1}, {T:canvas_1}, {U:canvas_1}, {V:canvas_1}, {W:canvas_1}, {X:canvas_1}, {Y:canvas_1}, and {a:canvas_1}. Complete the solids {FV|sFV:canvas_1} and {Oa|sOa:canvas_1}. [!just XI.11]

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_1" width="400" height="280" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="XI.34 diagram" src="propXI34b.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    // The second case: the standing sides oblique. Perpendiculars from the
    // eight upper corners meet the planes of the bases at S, T, U, V, W, X,
    // Y, a, making the upright solids FV (also BT) and Oa (also DX).
    //
    // Added, all transparent: faceless hexagons on the solids' outlines
    // (sAB, sCD, sFV, sOa), faceless quadrilaterals for the bases EH, NQ and
    // for FK, RO (twins of Joyce's filled top faces, which would be raised
    // over the figure if highlighted, #140), and the standing sides EF, AG,
    // HK, ON, DP, CM, QR (BL is Joyce's). The right angles at T and X are
