</script>
</figure>

I say that, in this case too, if the solids {AB|sAB} and {CD|sCD} are equal, then the bases are reciprocally proportional to the heights, that is, the base {EH|pEH} is to the base {NQ|pNQ} as the height of the solid {CD|sCD} to the height of the solid {AB|sAB}.

Since the solid {AB|sAB} equals the solid {CD|sCD}, and {AB|sAB} equals {BT|sFV}, for they are on the same base {FK|pFK} and of the same height, and the solid {CD|sCD} equals {DX|sOa}, for they are again on the same base {RO|pRO} and of the same height, therefore the solid {BT|sFV} also equals the solid {DX|sOa}. [!just XI.29, XI.30]

<div class="just">Above</div>

Therefore the base {FK|pFK} is to the base {OR|pRO} as the height of the solid {DX|sOa} is to the height of the solid {BT|sFV}. But the base {FK|pFK} equals the base {EH|pEH}, and the base {OR|pRO} equals the base {NQ|pNQ}, therefore the base {EH|pEH} is to the base {NQ|pNQ} as the height of the solid {DX|sOa} is to the height of the solid {BT|sFV}.

But the solids {DX|sOa} and {BT|sFV} and the solids {DC|sCD} and {BA|sAB} have the same heights respectively, therefore the base {EH|pEH} is to the base {NQ|pNQ} as the height of the solid {DC|sCD} is to the height of the solid {AB|sAB}.

Therefore in the parallelepipedal solids {AB|sAB} and {CD|sCD} the bases are reciprocally proportional to the heights.

<hr width="40%">

Next, in the parallelepipedal solids {AB|sAB} and {CD|sCD} let the bases be reciprocally proportional to the heights, that is, as the base {EH|pEH} is to the base {NQ|pNQ}, so let the height of the solid {CD|sCD} be to the height of the solid {AB|sAB}.

I say that the solid {AB|sAB} equals the solid {CD|sCD}.

With the same construction, since the base {EH|pEH} is to the base {NQ|pNQ} as the height of the solid {CD|sCD} is to the height of the solid {AB|sAB}, and the base {EH|pEH} equals the base {FK|pFK}, and {NQ|pNQ} equals {OR|pRO}, therefore the base {FK|pFK} is to the base {OR|pRO} as the height of the solid {CD|sCD} is to the height of the solid {AB|sAB}.

But the solids {AB|sAB} and {CD|sCD} and the solids {BT|sFV} and {DX|sOa} have the same heights respectively, therefore the base {FK|pFK} is to the base {OR|pRO} as the height of the solid {DX|sOa} is to the height of the solid {BT|sFV}.

<div class="just">Above</div>

Therefore in the parallelepipedal solids {BT|sFV} and {DX|sOa} the bases are reciprocally proportional to the heights. Therefore the solid {BT|sFV} equals the solid {DX|sOa}.

But {BT|sFV} equals {BA|sAB}, for they are on the same base {FK|pFK} and of the same height, and the solid {DX|sOa} equals the solid {DC|sCD}. Therefore the solid {AB|sAB} also equals the solid {CD|sCD}. [!just XI.29, XI.30]

Therefore, *in equal parallelepipedal solids the bases are reciprocally proportional to the heights; and those parallelepipedal solids in which the bases are reciprocally proportional to the heights are equal.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
----
guide:

A proof of this proposition could be made with very little regard to geometry but almost entirely in terms of abstract proportions. The volume of a parallelepiped is proportional to its base for equal heights (@XI.32), and proportional to its height for equal bases (not actually stated by Euclid), therefore the base and height are inversely proportional for equal volumes. Such a proof, although simpler, is not in Euclid's style.

This proposition is used in the proof of proposition @XII.9, an analogous statement about pyramids.
